I’ll inspect the pattern, metadata, and the framework’s analysis/tooling notes first, then identify candidate phases, build a GSAS-II refinement, and verify every exported artifact against the final project.

The chemistry is Ba–W–O from BaO₂ + WO₃ at 200 °C, but I’m keeping precursor/product mixtures and polymorphs open. The scan is raw Cu Kα reflection data (10–100° range, 8,301 points), so phase identification will be driven by the full profile rather than the nominal reaction alone.

Peak clustering already points strongly to an unreacted/partly unreacted precursor assemblage: the 23–25° triplet is characteristic of monoclinic WO₃, while an independent series near 27.1, 34.5, 42.2, and 55.9° is consistent with tetragonal BaO₂. I’m now testing every relevant Ba–O, W–O, and Ba–W–O COD structure quantitatively, including a shared angular shift and realistic broadening, before committing to the phase count.

I’ve ruled out BaO and BaWO₄ as major phases from their required peaks: BaWO₄ would demand a conspicuous reflection near 31.9°, and BaO a dominant line near 28.1°, neither of which is present. I’m proceeding with a two-phase WO₃ + BaO₂ Rietveld model and will use residual peaks to test whether any minor third phase is warranted.

The initial model is refining now with the exact Cu Kα doublet and Poisson weights. I’m staging background/scale, lattice and zero, instrumental breadth, phase-specific size/strain, then coordinates; this separation is important because the unknown calibration and specimen broadening are otherwise strongly correlated.

The physically bounded run exposed a local-minimum problem: several width parameters hit bounds while the fit was still moving, so those values are not trustworthy. I’m using the profile only as a screening diagnostic now and comparing all plausible WO₃ reference structures; the correct framework should fit without forcing the instrument or crystallite parameters to extremes.

The candidate comparison is still running, but one low-symmetry historical WO₃ entry has already failed numerically and is a poor model. The remaining candidates are the chemically plausible monoclinic/triclinic/orthorhombic variants; I’ll select on physically achievable profile agreement, not just the lowest unconstrained residual.

The best physical model is now stable at about 65 wt% WO₃ / 35 wt% BaO₂, but its residual has a clear sharp-core/broad-tail mismatch rather than unexplained reflections. I’m now separating Gaussian instrumental width from Lorentzian crystallite broadening and testing anisotropic strain; this is the main remaining route to a defensible fit and more stable fractions.

No third crystalline phase is indicated: the remaining residual follows the two identified phases’ peak shapes, not an independent peak set. The current model gives 65.1/34.9 wt% with only very weak texture and essentially no roughness/polarization correction. I’m doing one final apples-to-apples comparison between the triclinic and symmetry-related monoclinic WO₃ descriptions before export.

The unconstrained coordinate refinement was over-flexible (it shortened one W–O contact to 1.56 Å), so I’m not using it as the submitted structure. Restoring the published WO₃ coordinates costs only ~0.19 Rwp points and gives chemically normal contacts; I’m allowing only a small, bounded coordinate adjustment consistent with the stated reference uncertainty.

The phase assignment is stable: triclinic WO₃ plus tetragonal BaO₂, with no independent peak set supporting a third crystalline phase. I’m finalizing the constrained structural refinement, regenerating the profile directly from the saved GSAS-II project, and then checking that the CIFs, fractions, and reported curve all replay consistently.

The selected model uses only small, bounded coordinate shifts from database structures; this avoids the lower-Rwp but chemically over-flexible coordinate solution. The phase-scale result is essentially insensitive to that choice (about 65.4 wt% WO₃ and 34.6 wt% BaO₂ among the modeled crystalline phases).

One calibration issue remains important: an unconstrained cell/zero refinement let the BaO₂ cell absorb part of the specimen displacement, pushing it beyond the synthesis framework’s stated ±0.5% reference-cell perturbation. I’m testing the physically identifiable alternative now—joint zero and specimen-shift refinement with both cells bounded to that range—before locking the final structure.

The bounded calibration refinement is slower than the earlier unconstrained fit because GSAS-II is rebuilding the triclinic reflection list through several coupled cycles. The saved candidate remains intact; this is a verification branch to see whether specimen displacement can recover more defensible unit cells without materially degrading the profile.

That check exposed a more likely explanation than calibration alone: forcing the 1930s BaO₂ reference cell (3.78/6.77 Å) drives both zero and displacement to their limits and worsens Rwp to 23%. I’m checking whether the synthetic library contains a different BaO₂ entry near the refined 3.815/6.852 Å cell; choosing the correct reference is more defensible than forcing the wrong cell.

The calibration branch converged at Rwp 7.19% with the instrument zero fixed at +0.25° and a small specimen displacement of −79 µm. This reproduces the profile slightly better than the zero-only model, while keeping both angular corrections inside the documented ranges; their strong correlation will be reported as a lattice-parameter uncertainty rather than hidden.

The deliverables are now generated from a successful read-only replay of `final.gpx`; the replay recovered the same two phase scales and raised no negative-width, background, displacement-tensor, or polarization flags. I’m doing the final parser/CIF consistency checks now.

Completed. The refined crystalline composition is:

- Triclinic WO₃ (*P* -1): 65.46 wt%
- Tetragonal BaO₂ (*I*4/mmm): 34.54 wt%

Rwp = 7.190%, goodness of fit = 1.977. No crystalline BaWO₄ or third phase was required. Fractions are normalized over crystalline material; oxygen coordinates and lattice calibration retain systematic uncertainty.

